1
00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.603
In this lesson, you will learn

2
00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.606
what data agents are, how they work,

3
00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.609
and when they are considered trustworthy.

4
00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.611
Let's dive in.

5
00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:15.648
Let's begin with the definition of what
a data agent is.

6
00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:20.020
A data agent is an autonomous
or semi autonomous system

7
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:24.324
powered by large language models
that connects to data sources.

8
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:27.560
These could be databases, APIs, files,

9
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.398
streams, sensors, web APIs, etc.

10
00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:35.535
it understands queries
expressed in natural language or code

11
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.806
lands and performs actions
such as query decomposition,

12
00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:43.610
data retrieval, analysis,
or visualization.

13
00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:48.676
And finally, it provides insights
or takes decisions based on this data.

14
00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:52.919
For example,
it could send out an email with a report

15
00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:56.056
that summarizes the results of its work.

16
00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:59.526
Data agents are starting to gain
widespread adoption.

17
00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:03.163
For example,
many of you might be quite familiar

18
00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.599
with ChatGPT deep research system.

19
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.270
According to our abstraction
of what a data agent is,

20
00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:13.406
deep research
would be considered a data agent.

21
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:16.509
It only makes use of data

22
00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:21.381
that's available on the web
and its internal parametrized knowledge

23
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:25.418
to answer
open ended questions on various topics.

24
00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:30.585
In many enterprise settings,
it's useful to rely not just on web data,

25
00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:34.461
but in addition on data
that is available internally,

26
00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:37.730
either in the form of structured data

27
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.733
in database tables or unstructured data,

28
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:44.838
which could be in documents
and other multimodal formats.

29
00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:48.908
Snowflake intelligence
is one example of a data agent

30
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:53.146
that covers
this broader class of data sources.

31
00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.249
Let's now look at a very concrete example

32
00:01:56.349 --> 00:02:00.019
to understand
the scope of work for a data agent.

33
00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:04.891
Here's a very simple example
where a data agent is being asked

34
00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:09.762
to identify regulatory changes
in the financial services industry,

35
00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:14.929
and then an agent could, in this case,
find general trends in regulatory changes

36
00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:19.572
by examining data
that is available on the web.

37
00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:24.706
So such an agent could be a web search
agent that performs this task.

38
00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:28.882
Now let's make the query
a little more detailed.

39
00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:34.048
Now we are asking our data agent
to identify pending deals in industries

40
00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:38.491
that are currently experiencing
regulatory changes.

41
00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:42.362
Notice
that there are two parts to this question.

42
00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:46.866
First, identifying pending deals,
which is highlighted here in green,

43
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:52.033
requires access to internal proprietary
data, which could be in tables

44
00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:57.305
inside of an enterprise,
and a sub agent could examine or answer

45
00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:01.948
this part of the question
by making use of an agent

46
00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:06.586
that can take natural language text
and translate it into sequel.

47
00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:10.790
Run it over the database
and retrieve this information.

48
00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:14.227
The second part of the query,
which is highlighted here in blue,

49
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.131
is very similar to the first query
that we had on the previous slide.

50
00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:21.401
It's looking at asking for information
about industries

51
00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:24.404
that are currently experiencing
regulatory changes.

52
00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:28.942
So in this context,
the overall top level agent,

53
00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:33.112
once it collects these pending deals
by making use of the text

54
00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:36.983
to SQL sub agent,
can then make use of the web search sub

55
00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:41.621
agent to figure out which of these deals
are in industries that are currently

56
00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:45.725
experiencing regulatory changes,
and then synthesize a response

57
00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:50.530
that combines the results of calls
to these two sub agents.

58
00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:54.567
So now you can see the data
agent is starting to respond

59
00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:58.037
to more sophisticated queries that require

60
00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:01.207
making use of more than one data source.

61
00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:04.210
Finally, let's augment that query
some more.

62
00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:09.449
The first part up till here is the same
as the query we had on the previous slide.

63
00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:14.020
But now we are also asking the data agent
to help reposition

64
00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:16.856
our value proposition for each other.

65
00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:20.493
In the context of these deals,
given the regulatory changes.

66
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.797
So for that final part of the query,
once we have gotten the response

67
00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:28.034
from the agent that addresses
these first two parts,

68
00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:31.804
that the top level agent
can make use of another sub agent

69
00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:36.938
that does internal search over documents
available within the enterprise

70
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.781
to read the meeting notes
associated with these deals,

71
00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:45.518
and then synthesize a value proposition

72
00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:50.156
for each of these pending deals,
which takes into account

73
00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:53.559
the regulatory changes
in the corresponding industry.

74
00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:57.664
So we are finally at this point
where data agent makes

75
00:04:57.664 --> 00:05:02.664
use of three different sub
agents to answer a fairly complex query

76
00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:07.974
that addresses
all elements of this of this task.

77
00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:13.079
Now, let me give you a top level
overview of

78
00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:17.016
How would a data agent work
in order to accomplish a goal,

79
00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:20.086
such as answering the kind of query
we had on the previous slide?

80
00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:24.424
Agents will have associated goals
that they are trying to accomplish.

81
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.459
In the case of data agents,

82
00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:30.530
it could be responding to a query
like the one on the previous slide.

83
00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:33.900
It then creates a plan
to accomplish its goal,

84
00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:38.237
and then executes a set of actions
that are prescribed by the plan

85
00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:42.642
to achieve various subgoals
in the path of achieving the full goal.

86
00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:46.245
And here there can be iteration
between planning and action, where,

87
00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:51.217
depending on the results of the actions,
the agent might update its plan

88
00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:55.555
and then execute some more actions
and continue on this iterative process

89
00:05:55.722 --> 00:05:58.725
until it is satisfied
that the goal is reached.

90
00:05:58.725 --> 00:05:59.392
Well.

91
00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:02.028
So if we return back
to our running example,

92
00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:05.465
and this is also the example
that we will look at in

93
00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:09.602
some detail in subsequent lessons,
the goal is to answer this question.

94
00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:14.769
The planning step involves decomposing
this complex query into three queries.

95
00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:18.177
One on identifying pending deals,

96
00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:21.180
which involves calling the text
a SQL sub agent.

97
00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.218
One on understanding
which of these pending deals are in

98
00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:28.221
what industries,
what kind of regulatory changes

99
00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:33.126
are being experienced in those industries
which makes use of the web search agent.

100
00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:37.196
And finally,
making use of the internal search agent

101
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.330
to help reposition the value proposition
for each given that changes.

102
00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:45.471
So this is the form of planning.

103
00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.574
The first step of planning involves
this query decomposition.

104
00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:53.741
Then the execution or action
steps involves calling out to the text

105
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.851
the SQL agent, the web search agent,
and the internal search agent

106
00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:03.851
in that order to finally get to a point
where all the information is available

107
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.895
for the agent to synthesize
a good response that achieve its goals.

108
00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:13.800
Let's now get into what it means
for agents to work well.

109
00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:17.170
Given the structure
that I have just shared about how agents

110
00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:22.170
work involving this combination of setting
goals, planning and action to achieve

111
00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:26.512
those goals, trustworthy agents execute

112
00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:30.183
with goals, plans, and actions aligned.

113
00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:33.352
In other words, we want agents

114
00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:37.957
to have high GPA, our goal
planner, and action alignment.

115
00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.893
Let me elaborate on that
a little bit more.

116
00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:43.563
How do we measure an agent's GPA?

117
00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:46.799
The first step is to set goals

118
00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:49.836
for the agent in our setting with the data
agents.

119
00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:53.005
These goals
will often refer to the quality

120
00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:56.075
of the final response,
such as is that relevant?

121
00:07:56.242 --> 00:08:00.046
Is that grounded in retrieve results
as well as subgoals

122
00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:03.816
that the agent might be expected
to achieve along the way?

123
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.586
In terms of retrieving relevant results?

124
00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:08.087
To answer the final query.

125
00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:11.891
Once we have the goals specified,
the next steps involve

126
00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:15.495
ensuring a good amount of alignment
between goals

127
00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:19.499
and plans, lands
and actions, and actions goals.

128
00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:21.801
And we have a collection of evaluations

129
00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:26.801
that make use of judges
to check the agent's GPA.

130
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.409
The first evaluation is called
plan quality.

131
00:08:30.576 --> 00:08:34.747
Plan quality
sits at the interface of goal and plan,

132
00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:39.881
and it checks whether the agent's plan
is a good plan for achieving its goals.

133
00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:43.155
The plan need not be static.

134
00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:47.426
It might evolve over time
as new information becomes available

135
00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:52.131
to the agent, but the replanning steps
also need to be well justified

136
00:08:52.265 --> 00:08:57.265
given the information and observations
that the agent has access to.

137
00:08:57.603 --> 00:09:02.575
Next up is plan adherence, which sits
at the interface of planning and actions.

138
00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:06.479
Plan adherence checks
whether the agent's actions

139
00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:11.183
are in conformance
with are adhering to its plan.

140
00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:15.488
In other words, is the agent actually
acting in the way that it has planned

141
00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:16.255
to act?

142
00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:21.255
Deviations from the plan can be indicative
of failure modes as well,

143
00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:25.298
which are helpful for the developer
to be aware of

144
00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:28.568
and address
as the developer is building data agents.

145
00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.604
The third evaluation, also implemented

146
00:09:31.604 --> 00:09:34.707
with an alarm judge, is execution
efficiency.

147
00:09:34.874 --> 00:09:39.712
Execution efficiency sits at the interface
between goal and action

148
00:09:40.046 --> 00:09:43.449
sets in this portion of the Venn diagram,
and it checks

149
00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:47.987
whether the execution path
that the agent took,

150
00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:51.057
the sequence of actions
that it executed, was

151
00:09:51.057 --> 00:09:54.193
not the most efficient part
for achieving the goal.

152
00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.831
It can help identify redundancies
in the agent's planning process,

153
00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:02.702
which are sources for improvement.

154
00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:07.506
As we go through an iterative process
for developing data agents.

155
00:10:07.673 --> 00:10:11.510
Finally, logical consistency
sits at the intersection

156
00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.680
of all three of our GPA pillars,

157
00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:19.752
and it checks for inconsistencies
between the plan and the goal.

158
00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:23.189
For example, is the plan incorporating

159
00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:26.626
or suggesting steps that are inconsistent
with achieving the goal?

160
00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:31.993
Our sub goals, as well as inconsistencies
between planning and replanning steps,

161
00:10:32.098 --> 00:10:37.098
are between planning and action steps,
all of which sources of, incorrectness

162
00:10:38.037 --> 00:10:41.974
and opportunities
for improvement of the data agent.

163
00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:44.410
With that brief introduction,

164
00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:48.214
let me lay out the lesson plan
for the rest of the course.

165
00:10:48.381 --> 00:10:51.450
In lesson two, we will build a data agent

166
00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:56.355
that makes use of a web search sub
agent as a building block.

167
00:10:56.522 --> 00:10:57.957
In lesson three.

168
00:10:57.957 --> 00:11:02.957
We will expand that data agent
with capabilities for text to sequel

169
00:11:04.096 --> 00:11:07.967
as well as internal unstructured
data search.

170
00:11:08.134 --> 00:11:12.872
So the combination of lesson two
and less than three will help you build up

171
00:11:13.039 --> 00:11:17.309
in a notebook
a full agent that can answer the kinds

172
00:11:17.309 --> 00:11:21.447
of example queries
that we saw earlier in this lesson.

173
00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.718
And you will be introduced to,
the different steps

174
00:11:25.718 --> 00:11:29.522
that are involved in building
such an agent from scratch.

175
00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:33.125
We will make use of the line
graph open source framework

176
00:11:33.292 --> 00:11:35.394
for the purpose of building these agents.

177
00:11:35.394 --> 00:11:38.264
The general concepts, however, carry over

178
00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:41.067
to other frameworks
for building agents as well.

179
00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:45.571
In lesson four,
we will add support for tracing

180
00:11:45.738 --> 00:11:49.475
for instrumentation
and tracing of the data agent,

181
00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:52.545
as well as set up the evaluation

182
00:11:52.545 --> 00:11:55.548
for the goals associated with this agent.

183
00:11:55.614 --> 00:12:00.614
For this purpose, we will make use of True
Lens, which is an open source library

184
00:12:01.087 --> 00:12:05.925
for tracing and evaluation of agents
and apps in general.

185
00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:10.329
You will see specifically
how we can make use of open

186
00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:15.000
telemetry compatible tracing,
as well as specific kinds of goals

187
00:12:15.334 --> 00:12:19.305
that can be specified
well for data agents.

188
00:12:19.305 --> 00:12:20.406
But then through lens.

189
00:12:20.406 --> 00:12:23.876
In less than five,
we will expand the evaluation

190
00:12:24.043 --> 00:12:27.313
to measure the agent's GPA.

191
00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:32.480
This is where we will add evaluations
for plan quality, land adherence,

192
00:12:32.718 --> 00:12:36.455
logical consistency
and execution efficiency

193
00:12:36.622 --> 00:12:40.126
to the previously created evaluation
metrics.

194
00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:41.427
From lesson four.

195
00:12:41.427 --> 00:12:45.498
In less than five,
we will also show how agents can fail,

196
00:12:45.898 --> 00:12:49.034
what kinds of failure modes
these evaluations catch,

197
00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:54.201
and this will provide a basis
for developers

198
00:12:54.507 --> 00:12:59.507
to introduce improvements to the agent,
which will be the topic of lesson six,

199
00:13:00.312 --> 00:13:04.950
where we will look at specific mechanisms
that are informed by the evaluations.

200
00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.154
And lesson
five to improve the agent's GPA.

201
00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:13.320
Finally, we will summarize and conclude
the course with some key takeaways.
